{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>He shall purify himself with it.</b> With this ash. And if a questioner asks: how does a red cow purify the impure and defile the pure? One may answer: we find that one thing does two opposite effects — fire melts tin and other things, and hardens eggs and other things. And also medicinal herbs heal the sick and cause pain to the healthy — what is lax for one is astringent for another. Since this is so, one may consider through rational reasoning that it is possible that HaKadosh Baruch Hu commanded to purify these and to defile those. Also, it is found in a book: if water is placed in a white glass vessel in the heat of the sun, fire will issue from them." "<b>And if he does not purify himself.</b> What does Scripture come to teach? After it already says 'he shall purify himself with it on the third day and on the seventh day.' Rather, this is what it says: if he forgot and did not sprinkle upon him on the third day, he should sprinkle upon him two times on the seventh day, and it counts for him like the sprinkling of the third and the seventh. Scripture says <b>and if he does not purify himself</b> etc. — that three days are required between sprinkling and sprinkling."], :book :numbers, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The Aramaic phrases דרפי קמיט ליה ודקמיט רפי ליה are rendered literally: 'what is lax (רפי) for one is astringent/contracting (קמיט) for another.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:18:41.774602909Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}